2026-06-22 16:21:52,486 | AGENT | AIInnovation Agent received: The expected solution must provide intelligent assistance to agents, facilitating access to information, improving answer quality, and reducing processing times. What AI components can we design to meet these requirements, and how will our LLM stack support the solution's functional scope? Are there any known trade-offs or limitations we should consider when proposing our AI-powered solution?
2026-06-22 16:22:26,337 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/chat "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-06-22 16:22:26,337 | AGENT | AIInnovation Agent answered: To provide intelligent assistance to agents, we can design an AI component that utilizes a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) approach, leveraging our expertise in RAG capabilities. This would involve integrating a vector store such as Qdrant or Weaviate with a large language model like Mistral 7B Instruct, which is our preferred sovereign model due to its strong French-language performance and self-hostable nature.

Our LLM stack can support the solution's functional scope by providing features such as semantic search, question-answering, and text generation. We can fine-tune our models using methods like LoRA or QLoRA to adapt them to the specific domain and tone of the administrative services. Additionally, we can utilize quantization methods like INT8 or INT4 (GPTQ) to optimize model performance while reducing energy consumption.

However, we must consider known trade-offs and limitations when proposing our AI-powered solution. For instance, there is a trade-off between explicability and performance, as sovereign open-weight models like Mistral 7B may achieve slightly lower relevance scores compared to larger proprietary models. Nevertheless, we have accepted this trade-off for compliance reasons.

Another limitation is the need for offline quarterly fine-tuning on anonymized data due to GDPR constraints, which prohibits real-time learning from user data. We should also be mindful of the model size versus energy consumption trade-off, as our preferred model, Mistral 7B, with INT8 quantization uses approximately 80% less energy than larger models with comparable task performance.

Lastly, we should consider the RAG versus fine-tuning trade-off, reserving fine-tuning for domain tone and format adaptation while using RAG for frequently updated knowledge bases. By carefully weighing these trade-offs and limitations, we can design an effective AI-powered solution that meets the client's requirements and constraints while ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements and minimizing environmental impact.
